1
00:00:06,000 --> 00:00:12,082
Four and a half billion years ago, a cloud
of gas and dust collapsed to form our Sun.

2
00:00:12,162 --> 00:00:16,229
Around the new star,
the leftover material began to clump

3
00:00:16,309 --> 00:00:20,972
together into planets, moons,
and countless smaller worlds.

4
00:00:21,052 --> 00:00:24,769
Most of those smaller worlds
were swallowed by the planets,

5
00:00:24,849 --> 00:00:27,344
or flung out of the solar system.

6
00:00:27,424 --> 00:00:33,355
But some survived, almost unchanged,
for the entire age of the solar system.

7
00:00:33,435 --> 00:00:35,691
We call them asteroids.

8
00:00:35,841 --> 00:00:40,792
They are time capsules, carrying the
story of how our planets were built.

9
00:00:40,872 --> 00:00:45,448
And some of them are on a path
that may, one day, cross ours.

10
00:00:45,528 --> 00:00:48,652
This is the story of the
rocks that shaped our world,

11
00:00:48,732 --> 00:00:51,991
and of how we are
learning to watch over it.

12
00:00:54,341 --> 00:00:57,447
Between the orbits of
Mars and Jupiter lies a

13
00:00:57,527 --> 00:01:01,296
wide ring of rock called
the main asteroid belt.

14
00:01:01,376 --> 00:01:03,512
It holds millions of asteroids,

15
00:01:03,592 --> 00:01:07,824
from boulders to bodies
hundreds of kilometers across.

16
00:01:07,904 --> 00:01:12,594
Yet if you gathered all of them together,
they would weigh less than our Moon.

17
00:01:12,674 --> 00:01:14,757
The belt is not the crowded field of

18
00:01:14,837 --> 00:01:17,790
tumbling rocks you may
have seen in movies.

19
00:01:17,870 --> 00:01:21,385
The asteroids are so widely
scattered that spacecraft

20
00:01:21,465 --> 00:01:25,566
have crossed it many times
without a single close call.

21
00:01:25,646 --> 00:01:28,387
Some of them even have moons of their own.

22
00:01:28,467 --> 00:01:31,650
Why did these rocks never become a planet?

23
00:01:31,850 --> 00:01:34,102
The answer is Jupiter.

24
00:01:34,252 --> 00:01:37,596
Its enormous gravity stirred
the region again and again,

25
00:01:37,676 --> 00:01:42,266
until the rocks smashed into each
other instead of sticking together.

26
00:01:42,346 --> 00:01:47,733
Over time, Jupiter also carved gaps in
the belt, and its pull can still nudge

27
00:01:47,813 --> 00:01:53,879
asteroids onto new paths, some of them
leading toward the inner solar system.

28
00:01:55,729 --> 00:01:59,724
The two largest bodies in
the belt are Vesta and Ceres.

29
00:01:59,804 --> 00:02:04,656
In 2011, NASA's Dawn
spacecraft arrived at Vesta,

30
00:02:04,736 --> 00:02:09,132
a rocky world about 525 kilometers wide.

31
00:02:09,212 --> 00:02:12,962
Then Dawn did something no
spacecraft had done before.

32
00:02:13,042 --> 00:02:17,201
It left Vesta, and in 2015 it
entered orbit around Ceres,

33
00:02:17,281 --> 00:02:20,325
becoming the first spacecraft to orbit two

34
00:02:20,405 --> 00:02:24,196
different worlds beyond
the Earth-Moon system.

35
00:02:24,276 --> 00:02:27,402
Ceres is about 940 kilometers across,

36
00:02:27,482 --> 00:02:31,924
so big that it is classified
as a dwarf planet.

37
00:02:32,004 --> 00:02:36,565
It holds roughly a third of all
the mass in the asteroid belt.

38
00:02:36,645 --> 00:02:40,158
On its surface, Dawn found
bright patches of salt,

39
00:02:40,238 --> 00:02:45,279
left behind when salty water from
below seeped up and evaporated.

40
00:02:45,359 --> 00:02:50,108
Deep underground, Ceres may still
hold pockets of liquid brine.

41
00:02:50,188 --> 00:02:52,899
A cratered rock in the belt turned out to

42
00:02:52,979 --> 00:02:56,506
be a world with a
surprisingly active past.

43
00:02:58,356 --> 00:03:00,832
Not all asteroids stay in the belt.

44
00:03:00,912 --> 00:03:03,310
Those whose orbits bring them close to

45
00:03:03,390 --> 00:03:06,244
Earth's path are called
near-Earth asteroids,

46
00:03:06,324 --> 00:03:10,671
and astronomers have already
found tens of thousands of them.

47
00:03:10,751 --> 00:03:12,958
Most pass us harmlessly.

48
00:03:13,108 --> 00:03:18,320
But Earth has been hit before, and the
evidence is written across our planet.

49
00:03:18,400 --> 00:03:22,053
About 66 million years ago,
an asteroid roughly

50
00:03:22,133 --> 00:03:26,156
ten kilometers wide
struck what is now Mexico.

51
00:03:26,236 --> 00:03:29,893
The impact, and the changes
to climate that followed,

52
00:03:29,973 --> 00:03:33,164
helped wipe out the non-avian dinosaurs.

53
00:03:33,244 --> 00:03:36,334
Smaller impacts happen far more often.

54
00:03:36,484 --> 00:03:41,060
In Arizona, a crater 1.2
kilometers wide marks the place

55
00:03:41,140 --> 00:03:46,665
where a small metallic asteroid
struck about 50,000 years ago.

56
00:03:46,745 --> 00:03:52,415
In June 1908, an object perhaps fifty
meters wide exploded above Siberia,

57
00:03:52,495 --> 00:03:58,774
flattening some eighty million trees
across two thousand square kilometers.

58
00:03:58,854 --> 00:04:02,096
In 2013, a rock about twenty meters across

59
00:04:02,176 --> 00:04:06,185
exploded over the Russian
city of Chelyabinsk.

60
00:04:06,265 --> 00:04:08,865
The shockwave shattered windows across the

61
00:04:08,945 --> 00:04:12,186
city and injured more
than a thousand people.

62
00:04:12,266 --> 00:04:14,256
It was a clear warning.

63
00:04:14,406 --> 00:04:16,757
Fortunately, the largest asteroids,

64
00:04:16,837 --> 00:04:22,138
the ones that could change life on
Earth, are also the easiest to find.

65
00:04:22,218 --> 00:04:24,659
Astronomers believe they have found more

66
00:04:24,739 --> 00:04:27,369
than ninety percent of
near-Earth asteroids

67
00:04:27,449 --> 00:04:28,882
larger than a kilometer,

68
00:04:28,962 --> 00:04:33,114
and none of them threatens us
for at least the next century.

69
00:04:33,194 --> 00:04:39,143
The harder task is finding the smaller
ones, the size that could destroy a city.

70
00:04:41,493 --> 00:04:46,103
In 2016, NASA launched a
spacecraft on an unusual mission:

71
00:04:46,183 --> 00:04:51,243
to visit an asteroid, collect
a sample, and bring it home.

72
00:04:51,323 --> 00:04:55,957
Its name was OSIRIS-REx,
and its target was Bennu, a dark,

73
00:04:56,037 --> 00:05:00,552
spinning-top-shaped asteroid
about 500 meters wide.

74
00:05:00,632 --> 00:05:06,038
Bennu is a pile of rubble, held together
loosely by its own weak gravity.

75
00:05:06,118 --> 00:05:10,150
Soon after arriving,
the team saw something unexpected:

76
00:05:10,230 --> 00:05:13,440
Bennu was throwing small
pebbles into space,

77
00:05:13,520 --> 00:05:16,657
like a slow, quiet fireworks display.

78
00:05:16,737 --> 00:05:21,130
When the spacecraft touched
the surface in October 2020,

79
00:05:21,210 --> 00:05:25,654
its arm sank in, as if into
a pit of plastic balls.

80
00:05:25,734 --> 00:05:29,514
So much rock rushed into the
collector that some of it escaped,

81
00:05:29,594 --> 00:05:32,781
and the team had to hurry
to seal the capsule.

82
00:05:32,861 --> 00:05:38,477
In September 2023, a capsule
parachuted into the Utah desert,

83
00:05:38,557 --> 00:05:42,101
carrying about 120 grams of Bennu.

84
00:05:42,181 --> 00:05:48,188
Inside were ingredients that make life
possible: carbon-rich minerals, salts,

85
00:05:48,268 --> 00:05:54,250
and 14 of the 20 amino acids that
life on Earth uses to build proteins.

86
00:05:54,330 --> 00:05:58,342
That does not mean Bennu had life,
but it shows that the building

87
00:05:58,422 --> 00:06:02,632
blocks of life were scattered
through the early solar system.

88
00:06:02,712 --> 00:06:07,124
Bennu also has a small
chance of hitting Earth,

89
00:06:07,204 --> 00:06:11,780
about one in 2,700,
on a single day in 2182.

90
00:06:11,860 --> 00:06:14,473
Thanks to the mission,
we know Bennu's path

91
00:06:14,553 --> 00:06:18,033
better than that of
almost any other asteroid.

92
00:06:20,383 --> 00:06:23,377
Not every asteroid is a pile of rock.

93
00:06:23,457 --> 00:06:29,523
Psyche, in the outer belt, is about 220
kilometers wide and appears to be rich in

94
00:06:29,603 --> 00:06:35,968
metal, perhaps the exposed core of an
early planet that never finished forming.

95
00:06:36,048 --> 00:06:40,685
NASA launched a spacecraft
to Psyche in October 2023,

96
00:06:40,765 --> 00:06:43,984
and it is due to arrive in 2029.

97
00:06:44,064 --> 00:06:48,605
Another mission, called Lucy,
is heading for the Trojan asteroids,

98
00:06:48,685 --> 00:06:52,346
two swarms of rocks that
share Jupiter's orbit.

99
00:06:52,426 --> 00:06:55,292
On the way, it has already surprised us.

100
00:06:55,372 --> 00:06:59,541
In 2023 it flew past a tiny
asteroid named Dinkinesh,

101
00:06:59,621 --> 00:07:06,085
and found a pair of rocks, one of which
was itself two rocks stuck together.

102
00:07:06,165 --> 00:07:10,840
Each of these missions reads a
different chapter of the same story.

103
00:07:12,690 --> 00:07:16,587
Finding a dangerous asteroid
is only half the job.

104
00:07:16,667 --> 00:07:19,686
The other half is knowing
what to do about it.

105
00:07:19,766 --> 00:07:26,673
In September 2022, NASA tested the first
planetary defense technology in history.

106
00:07:26,753 --> 00:07:28,574
It was called DART.

107
00:07:28,724 --> 00:07:34,220
Its target was Dimorphos,
a small moonlet about 160 meters wide,

108
00:07:34,300 --> 00:07:38,243
orbiting a larger asteroid named Didymos.

109
00:07:38,323 --> 00:07:41,244
Neither asteroid posed
any danger to Earth.

110
00:07:41,324 --> 00:07:44,277
That is exactly why they were chosen.

111
00:07:44,357 --> 00:07:49,646
The plan was simple, and bold:
crash a spacecraft into Dimorphos at

112
00:07:49,726 --> 00:07:55,946
about 22,000 kilometers per hour,
and see whether its orbit would change.

113
00:07:56,026 --> 00:07:59,161
On September 26th, the spacecraft flew in,

114
00:07:59,241 --> 00:08:04,203
its camera sending back pictures
until the very last moment.

115
00:08:04,283 --> 00:08:08,092
Then the signal ended.
The impact was a success.

116
00:08:08,172 --> 00:08:11,612
Telescopes around the world
watched a long tail of

117
00:08:11,692 --> 00:08:15,653
dust and rock stream away
from Dimorphos for weeks.

118
00:08:15,733 --> 00:08:22,380
Before the impact, Dimorphos circled
Didymos every 11 hours and 55 minutes.

119
00:08:22,460 --> 00:08:25,604
Afterward, it took 32 minutes less.

120
00:08:25,754 --> 00:08:29,712
NASA had defined success
as a change of at least 73

121
00:08:29,792 --> 00:08:34,280
seconds. The result was
more than twenty times that.

122
00:08:34,360 --> 00:08:39,523
For the first time, humanity had
changed the motion of a celestial body.

123
00:08:39,603 --> 00:08:45,036
The lesson is clear: with enough warning,
we can push an asteroid out of the way.

124
00:08:45,116 --> 00:08:50,851
The key is time. A small push,
years in advance, becomes a wide miss.

125
00:08:53,201 --> 00:08:59,366
What did the impact leave behind? The
European Space Agency's Hera spacecraft,

126
00:08:59,446 --> 00:09:03,980
launched in October 2024,
is on its way to find out.

127
00:09:04,060 --> 00:09:07,360
It is due to reach Didymos
at the end of 2026,

128
00:09:07,440 --> 00:09:12,293
to measure the crater and weigh
Dimorphos for the first time.

129
00:09:12,373 --> 00:09:17,339
Meanwhile, NASA is building NEO
Surveyor, a space telescope designed

130
00:09:17,419 --> 00:09:23,058
to hunt for the dark asteroids that
are hardest to see from the ground.

131
00:09:23,138 --> 00:09:29,703
And in April 2029, an asteroid called
Apophis, about 340 meters wide,

132
00:09:29,783 --> 00:09:35,642
will pass Earth at a distance
of about 32,000 kilometers.

133
00:09:35,722 --> 00:09:39,599
That is closer than many of
our communications satellites.

134
00:09:39,679 --> 00:09:42,462
It will be bright enough
to see with the naked

135
00:09:42,542 --> 00:09:45,588
eye from parts of Europe,
Africa, and Asia.

136
00:09:45,668 --> 00:09:48,761
Astronomers have confirmed
that it will not hit us,

137
00:09:48,841 --> 00:09:53,797
and a spacecraft will follow it to
see how Earth's gravity changes it.

138
00:09:55,647 --> 00:09:59,272
For billions of years,
asteroids have shaped our world,

139
00:09:59,352 --> 00:10:04,641
carrying the ingredients of life,
and now and then, changing its history.

140
00:10:04,721 --> 00:10:08,645
The dinosaurs had no way to
look up and see what was coming.

141
00:10:08,725 --> 00:10:10,103
We do.

142
00:10:10,253 --> 00:10:13,577
For the first time in the
history of life on Earth,

143
00:10:13,657 --> 00:10:17,831
a species can see the danger,
understand it, and prepare.

144
00:10:17,911 --> 00:10:21,888
The rocks are still out there,
tumbling quietly around the Sun,

145
00:10:21,968 --> 00:10:25,364
as they have for four
and a half billion years.

146
00:10:25,444 --> 00:10:29,143
What has changed is that now,
we are watching back.
